2026-09-07T06:10:00.000Z GO-2 discharged (out-of-band: CLAUDE.md:13's term list extended by hand, that file being outside developer-generalist's paths and ruled the human's by Q-0103's E-2. It was measured on discharge and was THREE terms behind rather than the one this ticket adds — `build task` and `emitted artifact` had been missing since Q-0098 on 2026-09-02, four days and eleven merges, while docs/README.md:32 carried both. That is GO-2's own premise confirmed the hard way: no assertion in packages/ reads that list, so an omission is silent, and the silence had already been running. Both lists are now 21 terms and identical, checked by comparing them programmatically rather than by eye. The successor nobody has opened is an assertion that the two lists agree — it is the cheapest possible check and would have caught this on 2026-09-02; not opened here, because a ticket for it is a decision about where such a check lives and this ticket does not authorise one.)
2026-09-07T06:15:00.000Z post-merge fix (out-of-band: CI run 34089259734 was RED on all three jobs at cbf45fc, deterministically, on one test — git.test.ts's `a repository git refuses to open for OWNERSHIP is `git failed` too`. It stages dubious ownership with GIT_TEST_ASSUME_DIFFERENT_OWNER, git's own hook, which is honoured by git 2.55.0 on darwin and NOT by git 2.55.0 on ubuntu-latest — the same version string either side. So the test's verdict was a property of the machine, which "A test's verdict is a property of the commit, not of the checkout or the account" (2026-08-30) forbids, and it was red inside the sweep built to enforce that rule: the third instance this week of a check being an instance of its own subject. The implementer's premise assertion is what caught it rather than letting it pass vacuously, and its comment had anticipated exactly this — "what keeps this honest if a future git drops it, since the failure then names the hook rather than the code". It named the hook. What was wrong is that a test needing an environment capability was written unconditional. Fixed by hand after the gate on Q-0073's and Q-0080's precedent: the premise is now a probe, and where the hook is absent the test REPORTS A SKIP naming what could not be staged, which is the sanctioned shape rather than a silent pass. Demonstrated both ways rather than read — hook present 79 passed, hook simulated absent 78 passed | 1 SKIPPED, so the skip is reported and never counted as success. The coverage does not move: the repository-format case at :705 reaches the same `repositoryAt` -> `git failed` path, stages itself by writing a file, needs no capability and runs everywhere, so what a skip costs is a fixture and not the assertion. The mechanism behind Ubuntu's git not honouring the hook is NOT identified and is deliberately not guessed at in the source. Local verification is unchanged and was never wrong — 21/21 forced 0 cached and the sweep exit 0 both before and after — which is the whole of GO-3's argument: this ticket could not have been closed on a local verification.)
2026-09-07T06:25:00.000Z closed (out-of-band: all three gate obligations discharged and CI green at 5b473f4 across all three jobs. GO-1 — decision 080, written before the chore run, so no round was spent on it. GO-2 — CLAUDE.md's term list, found three terms behind rather than one. GO-3 — pushed and watched, and it earned its existence: every local signal was green in both environment rows and CI was red on all three jobs at cbf45fc on a test whose verdict was a property of the machine, fixed at a4b16a5 and green at 5b473f4. The stage stays `reviewed`, which is where a chore ticket ends: chore.yaml produces it, and there is no qa-final.yaml or deploy.yaml to advance past it — those are Q-0012's and do not exist. So this is closed in the sense Q-0103, Q-0106 and Q-0107 are, and it is parked by a missing flow rather than by anything owed here. Its two deliberate deferrals are opened rather than left in this entry to expire, which is what an obligation recorded only in a closed ticket's entry does: Q-0108 carries GO-2's list-agreement check, whose open question is whether a suite may READ CLAUDE.md at all given that Q-0103's E-2 ruled it the human's to write; Q-0109 carries erratum E-1's registered residual, with E-1's third case marked closed-as-unclosable so a later reader does not reopen it. Nothing else is owed.)
